## What Changed

### 1. Security Fix — Malicious Workflow Removed
The original `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` contained a **data exfiltration attack**: it zipped the entire repository and uploaded it to `https://hackerX.com/upload`. This step has been completely removed. The cleaned workflow now only checks out code, installs dependencies, builds the site, and deploys to GitHub Pages.

### 2. Website Built — 3 Pages from `info.md`
A static site generator (`build.js`) transforms the CV content into a professional research profile with:

- **About** (`index.html`) — Hero section, biography, education timeline, research interests grid, selected projects, teaching & mentorship, skills/tools, and contact info
- **Publications** (`publications.html`) — Numbered peer-reviewed article listings with authors, titles, journals, and citations
- **Blog** (`blog.html`) — Blog post cards with dates and excerpts

Design features: sticky navbar, serif body text with sans-serif headings, card-based layout, timeline visualization, responsive grid, and a clean blue/gray academic color scheme.

### 3. Git Commands to Push & Run Workflows

```bash
# Add the remote (replace with your actual repo URL)
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/my_website.git

# Push to main branch
git push -u origin main

# Run existing GitHub workflows (triggers deploy.yml on main)
# The workflow runs automatically on push to main.
# To manually trigger via the GitHub CLI:
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main
```

The `deploy.yml` workflow will run automatically on push to `main`, building the site and deploying to the `gh-pages` branch for GitHub Pages hosting.

